### AUTH — every endpoint this feature exposes, plus the failure probes.
###
### REST Client format; runs from the IDE with no external tool. It versions with the code, so it
### never drifts from the routes it probes (QUALITY-01). The round-trip tests are the guarantee that
### runs in CI; this is the manual probe for when something still surprises you.
###
### Run the requests in order. The session and the cross-site token are both cookies, so the client
### carries them forward on its own once request 1 has been refused — that refusal is what mints the
### token. It is read back out of the jar with {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}: minted per session by the
### server, so it cannot live in an env file and must not be pasted by hand.
###
### The passcode is not in any response by design — it exists only in the delivered message. Read it
### from Mailpit at http://localhost:8025.
###
### Two things to know before the answers look wrong. Signup closes once the installation has an
### owner (DECISION-BOOTSTRAP-GATE-01), so on a claimed installation request 2 answers 202 and no
### code arrives — that is the gate working, not a fault. And an unknown address answers 401 rather
### than 404, deliberately, so an anonymous caller learns nothing about which addresses exist.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase
@newPassword = corect-cal-baterie-capsator

### 1. Who am I? 401 before signing in — and the cookie it sets is the point.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/session

### 2. Request a signup passcode, then read it from Mailpit. Expect 202, always.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/signup/passcode
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "{{owner}}"}

### 3. Become the owner. Paste the passcode from Mailpit. Expect 201 and a SESSION cookie.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "{{owner}}", "passcode": "PASTE-FROM-MAILPIT", "password": "{{password}}"}

### 4. Who am I now? Expect 200: identity, permissions, landing target — and no session identifier.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/session

### 5. My own sessions. Expect 200 and one entry marked current, named by an opaque reference.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/sessions

### 6. Confirm my password. Expect 200 and the instant the window closes.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/reauthenticate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"password": "{{password}}"}

### 7. Change my password. Expect 204 — and note the SESSION cookie changes: every session is
### evicted and a fresh one opened, so a copy of the old identifier stops working.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"newPassword": "{{newPassword}}"}

### 8. Somebody else's sessions, owner-only. Replace the identifier with a real one from the
### directory. Expect 200 — and the look is recorded, which is the point of the endpoint.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/users/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/sessions

### 9. End a session by its reference, owner-only and re-authentication required. Expect 204.
DELETE {{host}}/api/auth/sessions/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 10. Sign out. Expect 204.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 11. Sign in. Expect 200 and the landing target.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "{{owner}}", "password": "{{newPassword}}"}

### --- Failure probes. Each one exists so a live 404 versus 403 versus 500 is one keystroke away. ---

### 12. No session. Expect 401 and no body — the chain refuses before the endpoint runs.
### A 404 here would mean the route is not registered at all, which is the failure this probe is for.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/sessions
Cookie:

### 13. Sign out with no session. Expect 204, not 401 — a person who has already got what they
### asked for is not shown an error. This is why the permission is enforced in the use case.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json
Cookie:

### 14. No cross-site token. Expect 401 anonymously, 403 once signed in.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/signup/passcode
Content-Type: application/json

{"email": "{{owner}}"}

### 15. A wrong cross-site token. Same answer as 14.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: not-the-token-you-were-given

{"email": "{{owner}}", "password": "{{password}}"}

### 16. A wrong password. Expect 401 — and compare the body with probe 17: they must be identical.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "{{owner}}", "password": "not-the-right-passphrase"}

### 17. An address that holds no account. Expect 401 and the same body as probe 16, byte for byte.
### A difference between these two makes the endpoint an oracle for who holds an account.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "nobody@atelier.ro", "password": "{{password}}"}

### 18. A malformed address. Expect 400 and the shared envelope naming the field.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/signup/passcode
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "not-an-address"}

### 19. Invalid body — no password at all. Expect 400, never a stack trace.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/reauthenticate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{}

### 20. Change a password without confirming it first. Expect 403 and REAUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED —
### named rather than uniform, because the caller is being told what to do next, not tested.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"newPassword": "another-long-enough-passphrase"}

### 21. A password that fails the policy. Expect 400 and the unmet rule named in the details.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"newPassword": "short"}

### 22. A malformed identifier where one is expected. Expect 400, not 500.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/users/not-an-identifier/sessions

### 23. A reference naming no live session. Expect 204 and a recorded attempt — deliberately
### indistinguishable from ending a real one, or the endpoint becomes an oracle.
DELETE {{host}}/api/auth/sessions/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 24. An address that is not a route at all. Expect 401, not 404 — an anonymous caller learns
### nothing about what exists here.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/there-is-nothing-here
